To resolve tailocin diversity, we used two approaches to assign hypothetical tail fiber (HTF) haplotypes: a local assembly method and a k-mer–based classifier with seven HTF reference haplotypes. Among the 40 historical ATUE5 genomes, 34 samples were successfully assigned: 21 showed consistent haplotype assignments by assembly and k-mer matching, while 13 were successfully assigned only by the k-mer approach (Supp fig 5A). PL0059 was the only sample with conflicting haplotype assignments (1245bp by local assembly; 1803bp by k-mer), likely due to coinfection, as supported by whole-genome k-mer profiles (Supp fig. 5). We kept PL0059 since both assigned HTF haplotypes are associated with OBC presence (Fig. 1C). The remaining six samples were not assigned with confidence: four were filtered out by both local assembly and k-mer quality filtering (≥65% of whole genome average k-mer depth), PL0053 was assembled as HTF_p7.G11 (1830 bp) but lacked sufficient k-mer support, and 64.GBR_1933b_S36 showed conflicting signals—partial, relatively low-depth mappings to the 1245 bp (p21.F9) HTF and TFA regions, alongside ambiguous reads mapping to the 1830 bp (p7.G11) haplotype (See GitHub). Given its stricter thresholds and higher resolution, we prioritized k-mer–based HTF haplotype assignments. 
